{"module":"afro-latino","voice":"female","duration":257.3,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"The African diaspora in Latin America and the Caribbean","start":0.0,"end":3.4,"words":[[0.05,0.19],[0.19,0.68],[0.68,1.39],[1.39,1.53],[1.53,1.88],[1.88,2.37],[2.37,2.58],[2.58,2.72],[2.72,3.28]]},{"el":"intro","text":"The U.S. received only a small share of the Africans carried across the Atlantic.","start":4.2,"end":8.89,"words":[[4.25,4.38],[4.38,4.7],[5.02,5.4],[5.4,5.72],[5.72,5.85],[5.85,6.1],[6.1,6.29],[6.29,6.42],[6.42,6.55],[6.55,7.06],[7.06,7.38],[7.38,7.7],[7.7,7.83],[7.83,8.4]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Most went to Brazil and the Caribbean, and their descendants live across the Americas, including millions of Afro-Latinos in the United States.","start":9.24,"end":18.87,"words":[[9.29,9.66],[9.66,9.96],[9.96,10.1],[10.1,10.55],[10.55,10.77],[10.77,10.92],[10.92,11.58],[11.84,12.06],[12.06,12.28],[12.28,13.02],[13.02,13.32],[13.32,13.68],[13.68,13.83],[13.83,14.5],[14.75,15.35],[15.35,15.86],[15.86,16.01],[16.01,16.97],[16.97,17.12],[17.12,17.26],[17.26,17.85],[17.85,18.37]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Racism there took different forms, but it was no less real.","start":19.22,"end":23.47,"words":[[19.27,19.88],[19.88,20.11],[20.11,20.33],[20.33,20.87],[20.87,21.25],[21.52,21.75],[21.75,21.9],[21.9,22.13],[22.13,22.36],[22.36,22.58],[22.58,22.97]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Where the ships went","start":23.82,"end":25.07,"words":[[23.87,24.11],[24.11,24.27],[24.27,24.6],[24.6,24.92]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Of the roughly 10.7 million Africans who survived the Middle Passage, about 388,000 were brought directly to mainland North America.","start":25.77,"end":35.63,"words":[[25.82,25.96],[25.96,26.11],[26.11,26.46],[26.46,27.39],[27.39,27.82],[27.82,28.4],[28.4,28.54],[28.54,29.04],[29.04,29.18],[29.18,29.54],[29.54,30.04],[30.29,30.65],[30.65,32.58],[32.58,32.73],[32.73,33.01],[33.01,33.66],[33.66,33.8],[33.8,34.37],[34.37,34.59],[34.59,35.16]]},{"el":"s0","text":"About 4.9 million were taken to Brazil, and most of the rest to the Caribbean and Spanish America (SlaveVoyages.org).","start":35.98,"end":43.95,"words":[[36.03,36.38],[36.38,37.16],[37.16,37.58],[37.58,37.73],[37.73,38.15],[38.15,38.29],[38.29,38.79],[39.04,39.25],[39.25,39.6],[39.6,39.74],[39.74,39.88],[39.88,40.17],[40.17,40.31],[40.31,40.45],[40.45,41.02],[41.02,41.23],[41.23,41.65],[41.65,42.15],[42.15,43.49]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Haiti and the Dominican Republic","start":44.85,"end":46.95,"words":[[44.91,45.27],[45.27,45.48],[45.48,45.62],[45.62,46.26],[46.26,46.83]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Haiti won its independence in 1804 and was punished for it (see “Before race”).","start":47.65,"end":53.34,"words":[[47.71,48.13],[48.13,48.39],[48.39,48.64],[48.64,49.56],[49.56,49.73],[49.73,50.49],[50.49,50.74],[50.74,50.99],[50.99,51.5],[51.5,51.67],[51.67,51.84],[51.84,52.0],[52.0,52.34],[52.34,52.76]]},{"el":"s1","text":"In October 1937, Dominican dictator Rafael Trujillo ordered soldiers to kill Haitians and Dominicans of Haitian descent in the border region; estimates of the dead range from about 12,000 to 30,000.","start":53.69,"end":67.99,"words":[[53.74,53.89],[53.89,54.4],[54.4,55.65],[55.91,56.57],[56.57,57.15],[57.15,57.67],[57.67,58.33],[58.33,58.62],[58.62,59.21],[59.21,59.35],[59.35,59.57],[59.57,60.09],[60.09,60.31],[60.31,61.04],[61.04,61.19],[61.19,61.63],[61.63,62.07],[62.07,62.22],[62.22,62.36],[62.36,62.66],[62.66,63.17],[63.43,64.01],[64.01,64.16],[64.16,64.31],[64.31,64.53],[64.53,64.97],[64.97,65.26],[65.26,65.63],[65.63,66.51],[66.51,66.65],[66.65,67.53]]},{"el":"s1","text":"It is remembered as the Parsley massacre.","start":68.34,"end":70.66,"words":[[68.37,68.5],[68.5,68.63],[68.63,69.14],[69.14,69.27],[69.27,69.4],[69.4,69.71],[69.71,70.16]]},{"el":"s1","text":"In 2013 the Dominican Constitutional Court ruled that children born in the country to undocumented foreign parents since 1929 were not citizens, leaving tens of thousands of Dominicans of Haitian descent without nationality.","start":71.01,"end":86.12,"words":[[71.07,71.21],[71.21,72.03],[72.03,72.17],[72.17,72.84],[72.84,73.87],[73.87,74.1],[74.1,74.39],[74.39,74.61],[74.61,75.2],[75.2,75.42],[75.42,75.57],[75.57,75.72],[75.72,76.16],[76.16,76.31],[76.31,77.27],[77.27,77.64],[77.64,78.23],[78.23,78.53],[78.53,79.78],[79.78,79.93],[79.93,80.15],[80.15,80.82],[81.07,81.44],[81.44,81.74],[81.74,81.89],[81.89,82.48],[82.48,82.63],[82.63,83.36],[83.36,83.51],[83.51,83.95],[83.95,84.4],[84.4,84.84],[84.84,85.65]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Cuba and Puerto Rico","start":87.02,"end":88.74,"words":[[87.02,87.41],[87.41,87.65],[87.65,88.19],[88.19,88.58]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Afro-Cuban veterans of the independence war founded the Partido Independiente de Color in 1908.","start":89.44,"end":95.37,"words":[[89.48,90.18],[90.18,90.69],[90.69,90.82],[90.82,90.94],[90.94,91.64],[91.64,91.77],[91.77,92.22],[92.22,92.34],[92.34,92.79],[92.79,93.55],[93.55,93.68],[93.68,93.94],[93.94,94.06],[94.06,94.89]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Cuba banned parties organized by race in 1910, and in 1912 the army crushed the party's protest, killing an estimated 2,000 to 6,000 Afro-Cubans.","start":95.72,"end":107.93,"words":[[95.77,96.18],[96.18,96.5],[96.5,96.9],[96.9,97.55],[97.55,97.79],[97.79,98.11],[98.11,98.27],[98.27,99.16],[99.44,99.68],[99.68,99.84],[99.84,100.81],[100.81,100.97],[100.97,101.21],[101.21,101.61],[101.61,101.78],[101.78,102.18],[102.18,102.9],[103.19,103.59],[103.59,103.75],[103.75,104.55],[104.55,105.28],[105.28,105.44],[105.44,106.33],[106.33,107.37]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Puerto Rico abolished slavery in 1873.","start":108.28,"end":111.95,"words":[[108.33,108.82],[108.82,109.17],[109.17,109.66],[109.66,110.22],[110.22,110.36],[110.36,111.49]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Puerto Rican-born Arturo Schomburg spent his life collecting books and documents of Black history; his collection became the core of the New York Public Library's Schomburg Center for Research in Black Culture.","start":112.3,"end":123.97,"words":[[112.32,112.8],[112.8,113.35],[113.35,113.83],[113.83,114.31],[114.31,114.66],[114.66,114.86],[114.86,115.14],[115.14,115.69],[115.69,115.97],[115.97,116.17],[116.17,116.86],[116.86,117.0],[117.0,117.28],[117.28,117.83],[118.07,118.28],[118.28,118.83],[118.83,119.24],[119.24,119.38],[119.38,119.52],[119.52,119.65],[119.65,119.79],[119.79,120.0],[120.0,120.21],[120.21,120.62],[120.62,121.24],[121.24,121.72],[121.72,122.07],[122.07,122.2],[122.2,122.62],[122.62,122.76],[122.76,123.03],[123.03,123.51]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Mexico and Central America","start":124.87,"end":127.41,"words":[[124.92,125.66],[125.66,125.93],[125.93,126.57],[126.57,127.21]]},{"el":"s3","text":"In the early 1600s, Gaspar Yanga led a community of people who had escaped slavery near Veracruz and fought Spanish troops to a standstill; Spain agreed to recognize a free town, today called Yanga.","start":128.11,"end":141.78,"words":[[128.16,128.31],[128.31,128.46],[128.46,128.69],[128.69,129.93],[130.2,130.58],[130.58,130.89],[130.89,131.12],[131.12,131.28],[131.28,131.97],[131.97,132.12],[132.12,132.51],[132.51,132.66],[132.66,132.89],[132.89,133.43],[133.43,134.05],[134.05,134.28],[134.28,134.9],[134.9,135.13],[135.13,135.36],[135.36,135.82],[135.82,136.21],[136.21,136.36],[136.36,136.51],[136.51,137.28],[137.55,137.94],[137.94,138.32],[138.32,138.48],[138.48,139.17],[139.17,139.33],[139.33,139.56],[139.56,139.94],[140.21,140.6],[140.6,140.9],[140.9,141.29]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Vicente Guerrero, a president of African and Indigenous descent, abolished slavery in Mexico in 1829, and thousands of people enslaved in Texas and the U.S. South escaped south to freedom.","start":142.13,"end":155.83,"words":[[142.17,142.68],[142.68,143.33],[143.58,143.72],[143.72,144.37],[144.37,144.52],[144.52,145.02],[145.02,145.24],[145.24,145.96],[145.96,146.47],[146.72,147.22],[147.22,147.8],[147.8,147.94],[147.94,148.52],[148.52,148.67],[148.67,149.82],[150.07,150.29],[150.29,150.87],[150.87,151.01],[151.01,151.37],[151.37,151.95],[151.95,152.09],[152.09,152.53],[152.53,152.74],[152.74,152.89],[152.89,153.25],[153.61,153.9],[153.9,154.4],[154.4,154.69],[154.69,154.84],[154.84,155.34]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Mexico counted its Afro-Mexican population for the first time in 2015 (about 1.4 million people) and recognized it in the constitution in 2019.","start":156.18,"end":167.14,"words":[[156.23,156.81],[156.81,157.32],[157.32,157.53],[157.53,158.47],[158.47,159.27],[159.27,159.41],[159.41,159.56],[159.56,159.85],[159.85,160.13],[160.13,160.28],[160.28,161.15],[161.15,161.51],[161.51,162.23],[162.23,162.66],[162.66,163.03],[163.28,163.5],[163.5,164.22],[164.22,164.36],[164.36,164.51],[164.51,164.65],[164.65,165.52],[165.52,165.66],[165.66,166.68]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The Garifuna, descendants of Africans and Indigenous Caribbeans, live along the coasts of Honduras, Belize, Guatemala and Nicaragua.","start":167.49,"end":177.78,"words":[[167.54,167.7],[167.7,168.39],[168.66,169.43],[169.43,169.58],[169.58,170.2],[170.2,170.43],[170.43,171.2],[171.2,171.96],[172.23,172.54],[172.54,172.85],[172.85,173.0],[173.0,173.46],[173.46,173.62],[173.62,174.46],[174.73,175.2],[175.46,176.16],[176.16,176.39],[176.39,177.23]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Brazil and the myth of “racial democracy”","start":178.68,"end":181.37,"words":[[178.73,179.22],[179.22,179.46],[179.46,179.62],[179.62,179.87],[179.87,180.03],[180.03,180.52],[180.52,181.25]]},{"el":"s4","text":"In the 1930s, sociologist Gilberto Freyre popularized the idea that Brazil, unlike the U.S., was a “racial democracy” where mixing had erased racism.","start":182.07,"end":192.96,"words":[[182.11,182.26],[182.26,182.4],[182.4,183.34],[183.6,184.61],[184.61,185.19],[185.19,185.55],[185.55,186.42],[186.42,186.56],[186.56,186.92],[186.92,187.14],[187.14,187.65],[187.9,188.33],[188.33,188.48],[188.48,188.84],[189.09,189.31],[189.31,189.46],[189.46,189.89],[189.89,190.54],[190.54,190.76],[190.76,191.19],[191.19,191.41],[191.41,191.84],[191.84,192.5]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The record contradicts it.","start":193.31,"end":194.97,"words":[[193.39,193.5],[193.5,193.78],[193.78,194.39],[194.39,194.56]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Brazil was the last country in the Americas to abolish slavery (1888), and afterward the government subsidized European immigration in a deliberate effort to “whiten” the population.","start":195.32,"end":207.19,"words":[[195.37,195.81],[195.81,196.03],[196.03,196.18],[196.18,196.47],[196.47,196.91],[196.91,197.05],[197.05,197.2],[197.2,197.78],[197.78,197.93],[197.93,198.37],[198.37,198.95],[198.95,199.97],[200.23,200.45],[200.45,200.96],[200.96,201.11],[201.11,201.76],[201.76,202.49],[202.49,203.15],[203.15,203.88],[203.88,204.03],[204.03,204.17],[204.17,204.83],[204.83,205.12],[205.12,205.27],[205.27,205.71],[205.71,205.85],[205.85,206.73]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Today most Brazilians identify as Black or brown, yet they remain far poorer and are far more likely to be killed by police.","start":207.54,"end":216.23,"words":[[207.58,208.0],[208.0,208.42],[208.42,209.26],[209.26,210.1],[210.1,210.27],[210.27,210.6],[210.6,210.69],[210.69,211.19],[211.49,211.74],[211.74,211.99],[211.99,212.49],[212.49,212.66],[212.66,213.08],[213.08,213.33],[213.33,213.42],[213.42,213.59],[213.59,213.75],[213.75,214.26],[214.26,214.43],[214.43,214.6],[214.6,214.93],[214.93,215.18],[215.18,215.69]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Brazil adopted racial quotas for federal universities in 2012.","start":216.58,"end":221.15,"words":[[216.63,217.04],[217.04,217.51],[217.51,217.91],[217.91,218.39],[218.39,218.52],[218.52,218.99],[218.99,219.73],[219.73,219.87],[219.87,220.68]]},{"el":"h5","text":"Afro-Latinos in the United States","start":222.05,"end":224.57,"words":[[222.1,223.09],[223.09,223.24],[223.24,223.39],[223.39,224.0],[224.0,224.46]]},{"el":"s5","text":"A 2022 Pew Research Center survey found about 12% of Latino adults in the U.S., roughly 6 million people, identify as Afro-Latino.","start":225.27,"end":235.3,"words":[[225.32,225.46],[225.46,226.46],[226.46,226.67],[226.67,227.1],[227.1,227.46],[227.46,227.82],[227.82,228.17],[228.17,228.53],[228.53,229.32],[229.32,229.46],[229.46,229.96],[229.96,230.39],[230.39,230.53],[230.53,230.67],[230.67,231.03],[231.28,231.64],[231.64,231.92],[231.92,232.35],[232.35,232.78],[233.03,233.74],[233.74,233.89],[233.89,234.82]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Many report discrimination both as Black people and as Latinos, including from other Latinos.","start":235.65,"end":242.07,"words":[[235.7,235.99],[235.99,236.36],[236.36,237.39],[237.39,237.68],[237.68,237.83],[237.83,238.12],[238.12,238.49],[238.49,238.71],[238.71,238.86],[238.86,239.52],[239.78,240.37],[240.37,240.66],[240.66,240.88],[240.88,241.54]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Colorism, the preference for lighter skin within a community, is part of this history across the Americas.","start":242.42,"end":249.52,"words":[[242.42,243.16],[243.42,243.57],[243.57,244.17],[244.17,244.32],[244.32,244.69],[244.69,244.99],[244.99,245.37],[245.37,245.52],[245.52,246.26],[246.52,246.67],[246.67,246.9],[246.9,247.05],[247.05,247.27],[247.27,247.79],[247.79,248.17],[248.17,248.32],[248.32,248.99]]},{"el":"s5","text":"The CAREN Act protects anyone reported to police because of race, color, ancestry or national origin.","start":249.87,"end":256.36,"words":[[249.92,250.06],[250.06,250.41],[250.41,250.62],[250.62,251.18],[251.18,251.6],[251.6,252.09],[252.09,252.23],[252.23,252.58],[252.58,252.93],[252.93,253.06],[253.06,253.41],[253.66,254.01],[254.25,254.81],[254.81,254.88],[254.88,255.37],[255.37,255.93]]}]}